### 2026-08-14T00:35:15.618Z | claude (sonnet) | user | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

> I guess until now rather than running a local server, I was just looking at file://[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO] -- is there any reason I shouldn't just do that?


### 2026-08-14T00:35:27.202Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

No real reason to stop — `file://` is fine for normal play. Two caveats:

1. **The service worker (offline/PWA support) won't register over `file://`** — `js/main.js` calls `navigator.serviceWorker.register(...)`, and browsers only allow that over `http(s)://` (or `localhost`). So offline install / PWA caching silently doesn't activate under `file://`. It won't error or block anything, it just means you're not testing that path.
2. It's also what the Playwright test suite uses (`http://localhost:8001`), so if you ever want your manual testing to match what the automated tests see, the server is the closer match.

For everyday play and most bug-hunting, `file://` is completely fine. Only reach for `http-server` when you're specifically dealing with service-worker/offline behavior or want parity with the test harness.


